    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Fourier transform works by treating any signal as a sum of different sine and cosine waves, essentially breaking a complex waveform into its fundamental frequency components. This helps engineers understand real-world phenomena because many physical systems—like sound generation, radio transmission, or image processing—are naturally built from combinations of these oscillating patterns. By shifting your perspective from time to frequency, you can easily identify dominant periodicities or smooth out noisy data, making it simpler to design filters or compressed systems.",
